    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 011203\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n345 AM PST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Widespread wetting rain continues through the afternoon. Locally \n  heavy rainfall and a few instances of flash flooding possible. \n\n* Conditions dry out on Monday as gusty north winds pick up across \n  the region. Temperatures remain slightly below-normal next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Saturday.\n\nAfter isolated stronger storms passed through the region last \nevening, the night brings widespread stratiform rain. Most rainfall \nrates look to stay light to moderate but there will be potential for \nlocally increased rain rates. Some of these increased rain rates \nlook to be focused on higher peaks as well as in portions of Mohave, \nLincoln, eastern Clark, and far eastern San Bernardino counties. The \ntrend of persistent rain will lead to some flood risk, but the \nmomentary increased rain rates over these areas will cause concern \nfor flash flooding. Into the mid morning, higher resolution models \nshow breaks in the rain but still offer scattered showers into the \nafternoon. Breezy to gusty winds will build as we begin to see some \nof those breaks in rain. Shower activity begins to erode into the \nlate afternoon and early evening with winds decreasing into the \nnight. The last line of showers could offer spotty storm chances \nbefore they exit into that night.\n\nRainfall totals will favor the more eastern areas with higher \nelevations in Mohave County looking to break 2 inches of rainfall \novernight. The rest of Mohave County and most of Lincoln County will \nrange between 0.75 and 1.50\", the Colorado River Valley looks to \nrange from 0.5 to 1.00\", Las Vegas will be 0.25 to 0.50\", and the \nrest of the region will be up to 0.50\".\n\nThe rest of the forecast looks calm and cool with some potential for \nfor windy conditions next weekend.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package... \nRain is expected to taper off and become more scattered by sunrise \nwith CIGs generally around 6kft; however, CIGs may briefly fall to \naround 4-5kft under any additional showers this morning. CIGs will \ngradually improve into the mid morning, as showers become become \nmore spotty and breezy southwest winds build. Southwest gusts \nincrease to around 25 kts this afternoon. Additional showers and a \nslight chance of thunderstorms will be possible this afternoon, \ngenerally between 22Z-01Z.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Rain will gradually \ntaper off this morning with isolated to scattered showers expected \nthrough the afternoon. CIGs will generally remain around 5kft or \nless through 15Z before increasing to around 6-10kft. Scattered \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms will again be possible this \nafternoon with CIGs potentially dropping to around 5kft. Southwest \nwinds 10 knots or less this morning, increasing to 10-20 knots this \nafternoon for most TAF sites. Winds at KBIH will shift to the \nnorthwest after 16Z and gust to around 25 knots through the \nafternoon. Clearing skies and lighter winds overnight. \n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Gorelow\n\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
